Max/OpenAI Design Standard
Version 1.0

1. User First

If the user has to read documentation to perform a basic task...

...redesign it.

2. Discover Instead of Configure

Software should find resources whenever possible.

Don't ask the user to type information the system can discover itself.

3. One Owner

Every responsibility has one owner.

One object owns touch.

One object owns music playback.

One object owns lighting.

Everything else communicates through well-defined interfaces.

That principle alone has eliminated a lot of complexity in MMPS.

4. Build Systems, Not Features

Don't ask:

How do we add moving lights?

Ask:

What service should moving lights belong to?

The feature naturally follows from the architecture.

5. Hide Complexity

Simple interface.

Powerful internals.

The customer never needs to know how difficult something was to build.

6. Everything Is Discoverable

This may be my favorite.

Mission Control shouldn't contain a list of stations.

Stations should introduce themselves.

That same principle works for almost any software.

7. Standardize Before Expanding

Before adding the tenth feature...

Make sure the first nine all work the same way.

Consistency is more valuable than quantity.

8. Build for the Future

Every decision should make the next feature easier to write.

Not just solve today's problem.

9. Test Like a Customer

We don't ask:

Does the script compile?

We ask:

What would the owner try first?

That subtle change has caught dozens of usability issues before they became real problems.

10. Ship with Pride

And finally...

If this were a commercial product, would I be proud to ship it?

That becomes the quality gate.
